1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,880
I found my own apartment key sitting on the lost-and-found counter, glowing scorching hot under the flickering fluorescent lighting.

2
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:19,020
For three exhausting years, I’ve cataloged forgotten objects inside this basement archive, but nobody ever comes back to claim them.

3
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:27,864
Every lost item carries a physical pulse, a lingering hum of heat left behind by the person who held it last.

4
00:00:28,164 --> 00:00:36,660
This morning, a silver pocket watch hummed so violently that its glass face shattered, pointing straight toward my timecard.

5
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:45,120
I flipped through the thick leather logbook to report the accident, only to find my own handwriting logging tomorrow’s inventory.

6
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:56,004
Driven by sudden panic, I sprinted down aisle nine, where rows of towering shelves held glowing coats, rings, and abandoned keys.

7
00:00:56,304 --> 00:01:03,096
At the end of the aisle sat a solitary wooden crate labeled with my full name and today’s precise date.

8
00:01:03,396 --> 00:01:12,660
Inside lay my favourite denim jacket, now ice-cold, tagged with a metal label that marked me as completely unclaimed.

9
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:21,768
I was never the clerk saving forgotten lives; I was the lost item, waiting three empty years for someone to remember.

10
00:01:22,068 --> 00:01:28,644
When you touch your warm apartment key tonight, remember that someone might still be waiting in the dark for you.
